
Ideas for future directions for this project
=======================================================


1. Some Abstractors have multiple instances running.
   The different instances are distinguished from each other
   by having different parameter settings or some other form 
   of biases. 

   These Abstractors have IDs. The higher level Abstractors 
   that consume their outputs sometimes consume multiple 
   outputs of the same type, to see which one will work best.

   Abstractors may become accustomed to the IDs of the lower-
   level Abstractors whose Abstractions work best for them.
   They have some way of becoming more closely coupled to those
   Abstractors.

   In this ways small networks of Abstractors can form and 
   possibly dissolve.



2. Bipolar Confidence
   Confidence values stored with Abstractions can contain 
   negative and positive confidence atthe same time.
   If an Abstraction has both high negative and high positive 
   confidence, it is said to have high Confusion.

   Abstractors actively seek out lower-level Abstractions or
   make changes in their own processing to reduce Confusion.



3. Marketplace
   The system runs on a machine that has limited resources.
   Resource usage is controlled by giving 'money' to Abstractors.
   They pay for lower-levels Abstractions that they use, and
   they are paid for their own outputs if a higher-level 
   Abstractor gets paid for its output that used their input.

   Bottom-level "Sensors" do not participate in the market.

   Top-level "Goal" Abstractors issue new money to their immediate
   suppliers when they are able to successfully post an Abstraction.

   Abstractors seek to reduce their costs by purchasing inputs 
   that are more likely to result in payment. They also seek to 
   increase their income by altering their parameters or by altering
   their behavior in some other way. Trying to produce what someone
   will buy.

   Abstractors that run low on money are forced to operate less 
   frequently or are terminated.

   Bankruptcy results in termination.



4. Antagonism
   An Abstractor decides that it is seeing a STOP sign up ahead, and 
   published its Abstraction with high positive Confidence.
   But another Abstractor looks at the STOP sign it has posted, and
   it posts an Abstraction that is somehow antagonistic. 
   For example, it says "That is not a STOP sign.  It is a man wearing
   a t-shirt with an image of a STOP sign printed on it." It also has
   high positive Confidence.

   A higher-level Abstractor takes both of these results into account,
   and makes its decision.

